My conclusion is that anything defined using `k_off_t` is forcing it to be 64bit wide. It means structs like the below will duplicated the RAM usage. I guess some check is needed to keep it 32-bit wide for 32-bit SoCs..
Edit: The below i just 1 case of this increase.. There might be other places as well..
```c
struct flash_area {
	/** ID number */
	uint8_t fa_id;
	uint16_t pad16;
	/** Start offset from the beginning of the flash device */
	k_off_t fa_off;
	/** Total size */
	size_t fa_size;
	/** Backing flash device */
	const struct device *fa_dev;
#if CONFIG_FLASH_MAP_LABELS
	/** Partition label if defined in DTS. Otherwise nullptr; */
	const char *fa_label;
#endif
};
```
```c
/**
 * @brief Structure for transfer flash sector boundaries
 *
 * This template is used for presentation of flash memory structure. It
 * consumes much less RAM than @ref flash_area
 */
struct flash_sector {
	/** Sector offset from the beginning of the flash device */
	k_off_t fs_off;
	/** Sector size in bytes */
	size_t fs_size;
};
```
